### The Numbers: A Steady Drumbeat of Recalls

As we pass mid-May 2026, the food safety landscape continues to unravel. With 419 recalls logged this year, the total is on track to set a new record if trends persist. Of these, 103 are Class I recalls, indicating a serious threat to public health where consumption could cause severe consequences or even death. For reference, Class II recalls account for 281, and Class III for 35 so far this year.

### The Repeat Offenders: Familiar Names, Familiar Offenses

Among the brands that have been flagged multiple times, Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC top the list. Both have racked up 11 recalls this year, with alarmingly high instances of Class I severity. Moonlight Packing's record-breaking score of 96 out of 100 (where lower scores are better) has firmly anchored them as the brand to scrutinize.

### This Week's Standout Incidents

- Undeclared allergens were a recurring theme this week, with Silver Moon LP dba Loard's Ice Cream recalling multiple products, including their "Loard's Peppermint Ice Cream" and "Loard's Champagne Sherbert." Both failed to disclose the presence of allergens such as milk and soy, along with artificial colorings like Red #40.

- California Dairies Inc. issued a Class I recall for their "Low Heat Non-Fat Dried Milk Powder," which could potentially be contaminated with Salmonella. The product was distributed under multiple labels and in different packaging sizes, adding complexity to the recall.

- An outbreak of E. coli O157:H7 was linked to "RAW FARM RAW CHEDDAR SIMPLY SHREDDED Original." This product stands out not just for the severity of the contamination but also due to the brand's marketing as 'raw' and 'pure,' which may mislead health-conscious consumers.

- Foreign object contamination was another alarming trend, with Walker Honey Co recalling their "Fain's Blackberry Honey Spread" due to potential stainless steel dust and plastic shreds. Meanwhile, Wehah Farms flagged their jasmine rice for similar reasons.

### The Brands to Watch

The stats reveal a compelling story. For instance, CHETAK NEW YORK LLC, although managing to avoid new recalls this week, remains a top offender historically with 52 recalls and a dismal Class I record of the same number. Meanwhile, Diva Fam Inc logged 14 recalls but dodged Class I infractions this year, showing some effort towards mitigating severe risks.

### Conclusions

While the sheer number of recalls is concerning, the repeat offenders point to systemic issues requiring regulation and oversight. Consumers would do well to stay informed, scrutinizing labels and sources before purchasing. As always, "Shelf Check" remains committed to bringing you the numbers and the stories behind the labels, free of spin but filled with facts.
